  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Well-specified as a minimal transport envelope, with strong dispatch and evolution behavior. But route leaks are about durable, peer-local policy scope and onward-restraint semantics, and those are deferred to higher-layer pCID-specific payload or wrapper protocols. This variant can carry a solution, but does not itself provide much of one.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit [pcid,payload] dispatch keeps layer responsibility clear.",
      "Deterministic CBOR bytes and no speculative parsing support stable signing and visible protocol mismatch.",
      "Unknown-pCID store/forward and wrapper-based evidence support sparse knowledge and protocol evolution without a central registry."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No native representation of route-policy scope, onward-restraint promises, or leak-violation evidence.",
      "Opaque payloads and pCID-specific handlers limit generic human/LLM auditability.",
      "No fixed signature or hop-evidence slot makes generic cross-protocol auditing harder."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "A peer may forward bytes it cannot semantically police, which is risky for policy-scoped routing.",
      "Policy promises may be buried in custom payload formats, weakening interoperable leak detection.",
      "Long-term audit depends on durable recovery of pCID meanings and wrapper semantics."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which payload or wrapper protocol would standardize route-scope and onward-restraint evidence?",
      "What peer-local records would prove that Bob leaked Alice's restricted transit offer?",
      "Should unknown-pCID store/forward be limited when policy enforcement depends on semantic understanding?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
